  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a compact upper level shortwave trough \nlifting northeast through the high plains of southeast \nColorado/southwest Kansas, responsible for a few ongoing \nisolated areas of sprinkles/light rain. Near the surface, a lee \nside trough of low pressure remains anchored in eastern \nColorado.\n\nRelatively dry conditions are likely early in the period \ndespite the SREF indicating another series of H5 vort maxima \nassociated with a weak perturbation lifting northeast out of the\nDesert Southwest into western Kansas late Monday night/early \nTuesday morning. Minimal moisture return will elevate surface \ndewpoints somewhat, but do little to bring about much in the way\nof instability. Still, a few pockets of sprinkles/light rain \ncannot be ruled out toward daybreak Tuesday as the \naforementioned shortwave trough interacts with a weak lee side \ntrough axis in vicinity of the Colorado line. Otherwise, expect \na drier pattern to persist through mid-week as ridging aloft \nbegins to develop and amplify across the Western High Plains.\n\nSeasonal temperatures are forecast today as prevailing southerlies \nhelp reinforce a milder air mass across the area, supporting H85 \ntemperatures holding around 10C in central Kansas to near 15C in \nextreme southwest Kansas. With the latest HREF painting a widespread \n80-90% probability of temperatures exceeding 65F to a 70-90% \nprobability of temperatures nudging above 70F near and along \nthe Oklahoma line in south central Kansas and much of southwest \nKansas, look for afternoon highs generally up into the upper \n60s(F) to the lower 70s(F). More widespread 70s(F) are expected \nTuesday as ridging aloft begins to develop and amplify across \nthe Western High Plains.",
